spuesta y country patriótico","book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","start":1965,"end":1968,"desc":"«Eve of Destruction», que definió y mató la «protesta» como género efímero, provocó una avalancha de discos de respuesta: «The Dawn of Correction» de los Spokesmen, «The Revolution Kind» de Sonny Bono, «What’s Come Over This World» de Billy Carr; y el «Universal Soldier» de Donovan, «The Universal Coward» de Jan Berry. El examen más claro de la mentalidad patriótica estaba en el country, que desde mediados de los sesenta se volvió reaccionario: un baluarte frente a los cambios y frente a una cultura juvenil incomprensible, blanco, de pueblo, de clase obrera y media baja (precisamente las clases más golpeadas por la guerra), con valores cristianos y patriotismo sin preguntas; George Wallace ya lo había usado en su campaña de 1964. «The Ballad of the Green Berets» fue su apoteosis; después llegaron «Day for Decision» de Johnny Sea y «Gallant Men» del senador Everett Dirksen.","color":"#a3e635","family":"Folk rock y canción protesta","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#country-patriotico"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":131765244780683,"artist":"Marty Robbins","artist_id":157213448084624,"title":"Private Wilson White","year":1966,"ess":0,"yt":"1vrB045QJXU","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"Discos de respuesta y country patriótico","scene_id":33854307482087,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#country-patriotico","chapters":["Marzo: The Ballad of the Green Berets: la guerra de Vietnam en Estados Unidos"],"pages":[[1,72],[1,496],[1,509]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":72,"text":"…for God, and Country, and You Mum (The Ballad of Vietnam)’ – a title that pithily summarised the hierarchy of many serving soldiers’ motivations. Marty Robbins’s January 1966 single ‘Private Wilson White’ was the story of a Vietnam hero who died to save his comrades: ‘As he yelled, “I died for freedom,” he…"}],"podcast":[],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Awards","body":"Won.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q41254"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"star on Hollywood Walk of Fame","body":"Won.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q17985761"}]}],"concerts":[],"xref":[{"kind":"xref","title":"Accidental Invention of the Fuzz Sound (1960)","body":"In late 1960, while playing guitar on Marty Robbins' song \"Don't Worry\" at Nashville's Quonset Hut studio, Grady Martin accidentally created the first recorded fuzz guitar sound. This occurred when he plugged his six-string baritone guitar directly into a recording console with a faulty transformer in one of its preamps, resulting in a distinct fuzzy sound. Though Marty Robbins initially disliked it, Martin, producer Owen Bradley, and engineer Glenn Snottie recognized its potential, leading to its inclusion in the hit song and subsequently inspiring the development of distortion pedals. \"A faulty transformer for one of the preamps inside the console halfway failed, making everything sound all fuzzy.\"","year":1960,"extra":{"from":"Grady Martin","from_id":95852745277301,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Unsung Heroes of Music: Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SdPPsmpgJg"}]},{"kind":"xref","title":"Commercialization of the Fuzz Pedal","body":"After hearing the distinctive fuzzy guitar sound on Marty Robbins' \"Don't Worry,\" Gibson Guitars approached Grady Martin to develop a commercial product. Martin, along with Glenn Snottie and Revis Hobbs, designed the circuit, and Gibson manufactured the first-ever fuzz pedal, the Maestro FZ1 Fuzz tone. This product made distortion widely available to guitarists. \"When the prototype was ready, Grady and Glenn Snottie took the contraption, which had taken on the form of a foot pedal, to Gibson's offices in Chicago. They took one listen and said, this is brilliant. We'll take it.\"","year":null,"extra":{"from":"Gibson","from_id":210905854082687,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Unsung Heroes of Music: Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SdPPsmpgJg"}]},{"kind":"xref","title":"Design of the First Fuzz Pedal","body":"Following the popularity of the fuzzy guitar sound on Marty Robbins' \"Don't Worry,\" Grady Martin was approached by Gibson Guitars. With assistance from engineer Glenn Snottie and WSM-TV engineer Revis Hobbs, Martin designed a circuit that could replicate this sound on demand. This collaboration led to the creation of the prototype for the first-ever commercial fuzz pedal, the Maestro FZ1 Fuzz tone. \"So with some help from Snottie and an engineer from Nashville's WSMTV named Revis Hobbs, Grady designed a circuit that would do just that.\"","year":null,"extra":{"from":"Grady Martin","from_id":95852745277301,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Unsung Heroes of Music: Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SdPPsmpgJg"}]}],"charts":[{"id":47174069689205,"type":"album","name":"Rock'n Roll'n Robbins: Marty Robbins Sings","list_id":26210831213670,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Rockabilly","src":"musthear","rank":149,"year":1956,"note":null,"yt":"8iYMXN3n__I","scaruffi":null},{"id":65097369034121,"type":"song","name":"A White Sport Coat (and a Pink Carnation)","work_year":1957,"list_id":231699243535749,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1957","src":"singles","rank":17,"year":1957,"note":"","yt":"zD8MnvyAi6I","scaruffi":null},{"id":36991527160710,"type":"album","name":"The Song of Robbins","list_id":277730079687595,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Honky Tonk","src":"musthear","rank":77,"year":1957,"note":null,"yt":"kSK